<body>
  <header>
    <p class="author">By Tahir Alakbarli</p>
    <h1>${page_title}</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A simple guide to chickens, their behavior, and the basics of raising them.</p>
  </header>

  <main>
    <figure>
      <img src="chickens.jpg" alt="A rooster and several chickens standing together">
      <figcaption>Rooster and chickens, circa 1905. History Trust of South Australia.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2>What Is a Chicken?</h2>
      <p>Chickens are among the most widespread domesticated animals in the world. The domestic chicken, <em>Gallus gallus domesticus</em>, descends mainly from the red junglefowl of Southeast Asia. People keep chickens for eggs, meat, and companionship.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How Chickens Behave</h2>
      <p>Chickens are active during the day and live in flocks. They form a social hierarchy commonly known as a pecking order. Chickens communicate using different calls and scratch the ground while searching for seeds, insects, and other small sources of food.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Feeding Chickens</h2>
      <p>Chickens are omnivores. While foraging, they may eat seeds, grass, leaves, insects, worms, and occasionally small animals. Domesticated chickens are commonly given prepared feed containing grains, protein, minerals, and vitamins.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Housing and Basic Care</h2>
      <p>Chickens need secure housing, suitable food, water, and enough room to perform their natural behaviors. A chicken coop can provide protection, nest boxes, and perches. Good ventilation is important, while outdoor access gives chickens opportunities to walk, forage, scratch, and exercise. Secure housing at night can also protect them from predators.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Quick Facts</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th>Scientific name</th>
            <td><em>Gallus gallus domesticus</em></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Main wild ancestor</th>
            <td>Red junglefowl</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Diet</th>
            <td>Omnivorous</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Typical lifespan</th>
            <td>Approximately 5–10 years</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Social structure</th>
            <td>Flocks with a pecking order</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Commonly kept for</th>
            <td>Eggs, meat, and companionship</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </main>

